<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Song of the Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">proposed dates range from the 13th to the 5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as שִׁירַת הַיָּם, Shirat HaYam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Sea, also known by its opening words as Az Yashir Moshe and by a later line as Mi Chamocha, is a poem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a> (15:1–18). In the biblical story the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> sang it after crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> in safety, celebrating their freedom after generations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and oppression in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; its first two verses are repeated in verses 20 and 21, where Miriam and other Israelite women sing them.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The song is commonly counted among the oldest texts of the Bible, and with the <b>Song of the Sea</b> in Exodus it is sometimes cited as the earliest example of Hebrew poetry.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-deborah">Song of Deborah</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Song of the Sea</b>, also known by its opening words as Az Yashir Moshe and by a later line as Mi Chamocha, is a poem in the Book of Exodus (15:1–18).</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a></li></ul></section>
